# IAX-8b — the registrar's side of a registration release (RFC 5456 §6.1.6,
# §9.4; notes §12(e)). Same shape as registration, REGREL in place of REGREQ:
#
#   REGREL  ==>          USERNAME                            §6.1.6
#           <== REGAUTH  challenge                           §6.1.3
#   REGREL  ==>          USERNAME + MD5 RESULT               §6.1.6, §8.6.15
#           <== REGACK                                       §6.1.4
#   ACK     ==>
#
# The time-stamps continue from the registration this release follows, but the
# sequence numbers restart: each exchange is its own transaction with its own
# call-number pair (see IAX2Registrar's note on per-exchange channels).

# REGAUTH — subclass 0x0e (§8.4).
#   000000c8    time-stamp 200 ms
#   00 01       OSeqno 0, ISeqno 1 (it has seen our REGREL, OSeqno 0)
# Payload:
#   06 06 "n0call"      USERNAME                             §8.6.6
#   0e 02 0002          AUTHMETHODS = MD5                    §8.6.13
#   0f 0a "9876543210"  CHALLENGE — a different challenge from the
#                       registration exchange, so a response computed over the
#                       wrong one would be visible                §8.6.14
8042 0001 000000c8 00 01 06 0e  06 06 6e30 6361 6c6c  0e 02 0002  0f 0a 39383736353433323130

# REGACK — subclass 0x0f (§8.4).
#   0000012c    time-stamp 300 ms
#   01 02       OSeqno 1, ISeqno 2 (our credentialed REGREL carried OSeqno 1)
# Payload: USERNAME only. §6.1.1's "MUST indicate the 'apparent address'" is
# written for the registration case; a release has no address or validity
# period left to report, and §9.4's flow shows a bare REGACK. Our parser
# tolerates its absence either way.
8042 0001 0000012c 01 02 06 0f  06 06 6e30 6361 6c6c
